{
  "meta": {
    "description": "Japanese verb conjugation system",
    "groups": ["group1", "group2", "group3"],
    "group_labels": {
      "group1": "Godan (u-verb) \u2014 five-row",
      "group2": "Ichidan (ru-verb) \u2014 one-row",
      "group3": "Irregular (\u3059\u308b / \u304f\u308b)"
    },
    "known_exceptions": [
      {
        "verb": "\u884c\u304f",
        "reading": "iku",
        "meaning": "to go",
        "group": "group1",
        "note": "The single most common irregular sound-change (\u9298\u4fbf) in the language. Every other \u304f-ending verb takes \u3044\u305f/\u3044\u3066 (e.g. \u66f8\u304f\u2192\u66f8\u3044\u305f), but \u884c\u304f takes \u3063\u305f/\u3063\u3066 instead: \u884c\u3063\u305f (not \u884c\u3044\u305f), \u884c\u3063\u3066 (not \u884c\u3044\u3066). This propagates into every form built on past-plain or te-form: \u884c\u3063\u305f\u3089 (tara), \u884c\u3063\u3066\u3044\u308b (progressive), etc. The original file's general \u304f\u2192\u3044\u305f/\u3044\u3066 rule is correct for every other verb \u2014 \u884c\u304f is the sole common exception and was missing from the source file."
      }
    ]
  },
